<p>The cards at the top of every tab show the machine right now: CPU temperature, RAM, clock, governor and (on a Pi) throttling. Below them appears whatever is running: a job with its output and a <b>cancel</b> button, downloads, and the chat model.</p>

<h2 id="start">first steps</h2>
<ol>
  <li><b>Compile llama.cpp</b> in <a href="/lab/system">system</a> → <i>build llama.cpp</i>. The chat needs a build; a run compiles the version its profile pins by itself. It takes a few minutes on a PC and longer on a Pi.</li>
  <li><b>Get a model</b> in <a href="/lab/models">models</a>: search (<code>qwen3 4b</code>) or paste a repo, pick a quantization that <b>fits</b> and press <b>download</b>. <b>Q4_K_M</b> is the usual first choice.</li>
  <li><b>Chat with it</b>: the <b>chat</b> button in its row loads it. Check that its answers make sense before measuring anything.</li>
  <li><b>Measure it</b> in <a href="/lab/test">test</a> → <b>new experiment</b>: give it a name, tick the model and the tests (speed, latency, Spanish writing), <b>create and open</b>, then <b>run</b>. The chat is unloaded while it measures.</li>
  <li><b>Read the results</b> in <a href="/">results</a>, or press <b>guide</b> in the test tab to get the winning configuration and its <code>llama-server</code> command.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="concepts">concepts</h2>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>model, GGUF</b></td><td>llama.cpp runs models in the GGUF format. A Hugging Face repo without <code>.gguf</code> files cannot run as is; most models have a GGUF version (unsloth, bartowski, the model's own org…).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>quantization</b></td><td>How many bits each weight keeps: <code>Q8_0</code> is close to the original and twice the size of <code>Q4_K_M</code>, which is smaller and faster and loses a little quality. <code>IQ</code> and <code>UD-</code> variants squeeze further. Bigger file = more RAM and usually slower generation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>where models live</b></td><td><b>cache</b> is the Hugging Face cache; <b>★ models/</b> are favorites, never deleted automatically. The <code>pi5</code> profile removes a cached model after its last run (little disk); experiments created in the lab set <code>retention: keep</code>, so they never delete your models.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>build</b></td><td>A compiled llama.cpp, identified by its commit and backend (cpu, cuda, vulkan…). Results record the exact build.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>profile</b></td><td>A file in <code>experiments/_profiles/</code> that an experiment <code>extends</code>: the build and how to treat the hardware. <code>pc</code> pins llama.cpp <code>b11507</code>. <code>pi5</code> builds <code>LLB_LLAMA_REF</code> (default <code>master</code>: pin a tag for repeatable series), waits for the CPU to cool to 60 °C between runs, refuses to start while throttled and needs the <code>performance</code> governor. <code>deterministic</code> sets temperature 0 and a fixed seed, so answers repeat.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>experiment</b></td><td>One YAML in <code>experiments/</code>: models × builds × settings, the phases to measure, and optionally gates. Its <code>experiment.name</code> is the results folder.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>defaults, matrix</b></td><td><b>defaults</b> are the llama.cpp settings every run uses. The <b>matrix</b> lists settings to vary, e.g. <code>load.threads: [2, 4]</code>; axes are crossed (2 × 3 = 6 variants), and <code>zip</code> moves several together.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>configuration, run</b></td><td>A configuration is one build × model × variant. A run is one measurement of one configuration in one phase (and suite).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>phases</b></td><td><b>bench</b>: raw speed with <code>llama-bench</code>, no suite. <b>latency</b>: one request at a time through <code>llama-server</code> (usually synthetic suites). <b>quality</b>: answers scored by the suite's evaluators. <b>serving</b>: several clients at once. <b>lm_eval</b>: standard benchmarks (extra <code>lm-eval</code>). <b>judge</b>: a stronger model rates answers 1–5, stored apart.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>suite, item, evaluator</b></td><td>A suite (<code>suites/*.yaml</code>) is a list of requests; an item is one request; an evaluator is the check that scores its answer from 0 to 1 and decides pass or fail. Suites can also be synthetic (exact token counts), rows of a file, or a Hugging Face dataset.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>gates, select</b></td><td>Gates are pass/fail thresholds on a metric (e.g. <code>tps:tg128 &gt;= 10</code>). <b>select</b> says which metric picks the winner among those that pass. <b>guide</b> applies both.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>fingerprint, resume</b></td><td>Each run has a fingerprint of everything that determines the measurement. <code>--resume</code> skips runs whose fingerprint already completed, so an interrupted experiment continues where it stopped, and changing a setting measures only what it affects.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="recipes">recipes</h2>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>Is this model fast enough to chat with on my machine?</h3>
  <p>In <a href="/lab/test">test</a> → <b>new experiment</b>, tick the model and the <b>speed</b> and <b>latency</b> tests, then <b>run</b>. In results look at <b>tg128</b> (tokens/s while writing; roughly 8–10 tokens/s already reads comfortably) and <b>TTFT p95</b> (the wait before the first word, in ms).</p>
</div>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>Q4_K_M or Q8_0?</h3>
  <p>Download both quantizations, then a <b>new experiment</b> with both models ticked and the speed and Spanish writing tests. Compare tokens/s, peak memory and score: if the score barely changes, the smaller one wins. The <code>quantization.yaml</code> example compares four.</p>
</div>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>How many threads?</h3>
  <p>Open an experiment, and in <b>matrix</b> add the axis <code>load.threads</code> with values like <code>2, 3, 4</code>. Every value becomes a configuration. On a Pi 5 the best value is often below the core count (see <code>pi5-threads.yaml</code>).</p>
</div>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>Does it write good Spanish? With my own questions?</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>In <a href="/lab/suites">suites</a> → <b>new…</b> → <i>Spanish prompts with checks</i>. Replace the example items with yours; the <b>evaluator cheat sheet</b> says which check fits each case.</li>
    <li>Load a model in the chat and press <b>try it</b>: each answer appears with its score. Adjust the checks until they judge the way you would.</li>
    <li><b>use in an experiment →</b> opens the test tab with your suite ticked: name it, tick the models, <b>create and open</b>, <b>run</b>.</li>
    <li>For grammar or naturalness, which no rule can check, add an <code>llm_judge</code> evaluator and a <b>judge</b> phase, then press <b>judge</b> after the run.</li>
  </ol>
</div>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>Deploy the winner</h3>
  <p>Add gates (e.g. <code>tps:tg128 &gt;= 10</code>) and a <b>select</b> to the experiment, then press <b>guide</b>: it lists which configurations pass, picks the winner and writes its <code>llama-server</code> command and a systemd unit to <code>guidance.md</code>.</p>
</div>
<div class="recipe">
  <h3>Compare the PC with the Pi</h3>
  <p>Run the same experiment on both machines (with each machine's profile). In <a href="/">results</a>, tick both experiments and press <b>compare</b>: configurations side by side, machine differences highlighted.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="phases">what it measures</h2>
<p>An experiment enables one or more phases:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>phase</th><th>runs on</th><th>measures</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>bench</code></td><td><code>llama-bench</code></td><td>Prompt and generation tokens/s per test (<code>pp512</code>, <code>tg128</code>…), with median, IQR, CV and a 95% CI. On unless <code>enabled: false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>latency</code></td><td><code>llama-server</code></td><td>Time to first token, inter-token latency, end-to-end latency, one request at a time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>quality</code></td><td><code>llama-server</code></td><td>A score per answer from deterministic evaluators (exact, contains, regex, numeric, length, JSON schema…). It also covers embeddings, reranking and vision.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>serving</code></td><td><code>llama-server</code></td><td>Concurrent clients: requests/s, aggregate tokens/s, p95 latencies, errors.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>lm_eval</code></td><td>lm-evaluation-harness</td><td>Standard benchmarks such as GSM8K (extra <code>lm-eval</code>).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>judge</code></td><td><code>llama-server</code></td><td>A stronger LLM scores answers from 1 to 5, stored apart from the objective scores.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>During every run llb also records CPU temperature, frequency, RAM, the peak memory of the llama.cpp process and, on a Pi, throttling.</p>

<h2 id="writing">writing an experiment</h2>
<p>The test tab's form writes this YAML for you; this is what a hand-written one looks like:</p>
<pre data-example="experiment">version: 1
extends: [_profiles/pc.yaml]                # build + hardware settings (or _profiles/pi5.yaml)

experiment:
  name: laptop-threads                      # also the results folder name

models:
  - { id: qwen3-1.7b, source: "hf://unsloth/Qwen3-1.7B-GGUF:Q4_K_M" }

defaults:                                   # llama.cpp settings shared by every run
  load: { ctx_size: 4096, batch_size: 512, flash_attn: auto }

matrix:                                     # what varies: axes are crossed
  load.threads: [2, 4, 6, 8]
  zip:                                      # these move together: f16/f16, then q8_0/q8_0
    - kv.cache_type_k: [f16, q8_0]
      kv.cache_type_v: [f16, q8_0]

phases:
  bench: { n_prompt: [512], n_gen: [128], repetitions: 5 }
  latency: { enabled: true, suites: [../suites/synthetic-512x128.yaml] }

gates:                                      # pass/fail per configuration
  - { metric: "tps:tg128", op: "&gt;=", value: 10 }
  - { metric: "ttft_ms_p95@synthetic-512x128", op: "&lt;=", value: 2000, agg: max }
select: { metric: "tps:tg128", objective: maximize }</pre>
<p>Press <b>validate</b> (or run <code>llb validate &lt;yaml&gt;</code>) after each change: it points at the exact field that is wrong.</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Settings</b>: the groups under <code>defaults</code> mirror llama.cpp's options: <code>load</code>, <code>kv</code>, <code>gpu</code>, <code>rope</code>, <code>speculative</code>, <code>server</code>, <code>sampling</code>. Raw flags go in <code>extra_args</code> and environment variables in <code>env</code>. A field a tool doesn't support is reported as <code>ignored</code> (for example, <code>llama-bench</code> has no <code>ctx_size</code>).</li>
  <li><b>Reasoning</b>: <code>defaults.server.reasoning</code> (<code>on</code>, <code>off</code> or <code>auto</code>), <code>reasoning_budget</code> and <code>reasoning_format</code> control thinking in models such as Qwen3, and <code>chat_template_kwargs</code> passes options like <code>{ enable_thinking: false }</code> to the template. Thinking tokens count towards a suite's <code>max_tokens</code> and delay the visible answer, so they change both latency and quality. The reasoning text is stored apart from the answer.</li>
  <li><b>Matrix</b>: <code>exclude</code> removes combinations and <code>include</code> adds them. Every model and build is crossed with every variant.</li>
  <li><b>Models</b>: <code>source</code> is <code>hf://org/repo:QUANT</code> (optionally pinned with <code>@&lt;commit&gt;</code> after the repo), <code>hf://org/repo/path/file.gguf</code> or a local <code>.gguf</code>. <code>retention: keep</code> or <code>delete_after_run</code> overrides the machine's <code>hardware.model_retention</code>. For gated repos, set <code>HF_TOKEN</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Builds</b>: <code>llama_cpp.builds</code> takes either <code>{ id, ref, backend }</code>, which llb compiles and caches (<code>cpu</code>, <code>cuda</code>, <code>vulkan</code>, <code>metal</code>, <code>blas</code>), or <code>{ id, bin_dir }</code> to use binaries you already have. With several builds you can compare llama.cpp versions.</li>
  <li><b>Suites</b>: the requests for the server phases live in <code>suites/</code>: synthetic prompts with exact token counts, Spanish writing tasks, GSM8K from Hugging Face, embeddings, reranking and vision. A suite can also read jsonl/csv/parquet files or a Hugging Face dataset.</li>
  <li><b>Gate metrics</b>: <code>tps:&lt;test&gt;</code> and <code>tps_ci_low:&lt;test&gt;</code>; any server metric (<code>ttft_ms_p95</code>, <code>gen_tps_mean</code>, <code>score_mean</code>, <code>error_rate</code>…), optionally restricted to one suite with <code>@&lt;suite&gt;</code>; <code>peak_rss_mb</code>, <code>temp_peak_c</code> and <code>throttled</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Variables</b>: <code>${env:VAR:-default}</code> reads an environment variable. The profiles use it for <code>LLB_LLAMA_REF</code>, the llama.cpp version.</li>
</ul>

