<h2 id="commands">commands</h2>
<p>Every button in the lab runs one of these; from a terminal, run them as <code>uv run llb …</code> from the repository root. Every command has <code>--help</code>.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>llb validate &lt;yaml&gt;</code></td><td>Checks the schema, the suites, the models and the flags against each build.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb plan &lt;yaml&gt;</code></td><td>Shows what <code>run</code> would execute.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb run &lt;yaml&gt; [--resume] [-m &lt;model&gt;] [-y]</code></td><td>Runs the experiment. Exits with 1 if a run failed and 130 if interrupted.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb report &lt;yaml&gt; [-f html -f md -f png]</code></td><td>Builds reports from stored results.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb guide &lt;yaml&gt;</code></td><td>Applies the gates, picks the winner and writes <code>guidance.md</code>. Exits with 1 if nothing passes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb judge &lt;yaml&gt;</code></td><td>Runs the LLM judge over pending answers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb serve</code></td><td>The read-only results dashboard on its own.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb lab [--llama-server &lt;bin&gt;] [--experiments &lt;dir&gt;]</code></td><td>This lab.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb llama build --ref &lt;tag&gt; [--backend cuda]</code>, <code>llama ls</code></td><td>Compiles llama.cpp and lists the builds.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb models pull|ls|inspect|favorite|unfavorite|prune</code></td><td>Manages GGUF models.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>llb doctor</code></td><td>Shows the environment.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="files">results and resuming</h2>
<p>Each experiment writes to <code>$LLB_HOME/runs/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, or to <code>output.directory</code> if you set it:</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>benchmark.sqlite</code></td><td>All results, with their provenance (llama.cpp commit, model sha256, flags, machine).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>summary.csv</code></td><td>llama-bench results.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>latency.csv</code>, <code>quality.csv</code>, <code>serving.csv</code></td><td>Server-phase results.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>runs.jsonl</code></td><td>Every run with its full configuration and requests.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>report.*</code></td><td>Reports.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>guidance.md</code></td><td>Gates, winner and deploy command.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A failed run never stops the experiment: its error is stored and the rest continue. <code>--resume</code> skips every run whose fingerprint already completed.</p>

<h2 id="models">models and disk space</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Downloaded models live in the Hugging Face cache (<code>HF_HOME</code>). Favorites live in <code>models/</code> as real files that are never deleted automatically; you can drop your own <code>.gguf</code> files there too.</li>
  <li>A model is looked up in <code>models/</code> first, then in the cache, and is downloaded only if it is in neither.</li>
  <li><code>hardware.model_retention</code> says whether downloaded models stay in the cache. The <code>pc</code> profile keeps them; <code>pi5</code> uses <code>delete_after_run</code>, so a Pi holds about one model at a time.</li>
  <li><b>inspect</b> (or <code>llb models inspect</code>) reads the GGUF header of a model already on disk: architecture, size, quantization, parameters, context length, layers, heads and, for mixture-of-experts models, the expert counts. In the raw keys you can tell <b>reasoning</b> (<code>tokenizer.chat_template</code> mentions <code>&lt;think&gt;</code> or <code>enable_thinking</code>), <b>base or chat</b> (a base model usually has no chat template), <b>embeddings or reranking</b> (<code>&lt;architecture&gt;.pooling_type</code> is set); <b>vision</b> needs a separate <code>mmproj-*.gguf</code> from the repo.</li>
</ul>
<pre>llb models pull hf://unsloth/Qwen3-1.7B-GGUF:Q4_K_M --favorite
llb models prune --older-than 30 --dry-run          # free cache space</pre>

<h2 id="numbers">reading the numbers</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>metric</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>tps:pp512</code></td><td>Prompt processing: tokens/s reading a 512-token prompt. What you wait for before the first word on long inputs. Higher is better.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tps:tg128</code></td><td>Text generation: tokens/s writing 128 tokens. The reading speed of the answer. Higher is better.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>95% CI, cv</td><td>The interval where the true mean most likely lies, and the spread of the repetitions. A run is marked <b>unstable</b> when cv exceeds 5% or the Pi throttled: repeat it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ttft_ms_p50 / p95</code></td><td>Time to first token: from sending the request to the first word, typical (p50) and near-worst (p95) case.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>itl_ms_p50 / p95</code></td><td>Inter-token latency: the gap between words while it writes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>e2e_ms_p50 / p95</code></td><td>The whole request, start to last token.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>gen_tps_mean</code>, <code>prompt_tps_mean</code></td><td>Writing and reading speed measured through llama-server.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>requests_per_s</code>, <code>gen_tps_aggregate</code></td><td>Serving: what several concurrent clients get in total.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>score_mean</code>, <code>pass_rate</code></td><td>Quality: mean evaluator score (0–1) and share of answers that pass.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>requests_failed</code>, <code>error_rate</code></td><td>Requests that errored or timed out.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>peak_rss_mb</code></td><td>Peak memory of the llama.cpp process: weights + KV cache + buffers. It must fit in RAM.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>temp_peak_c</code>, <code>throttled</code></td><td>Hottest CPU temperature during the run, and whether a Pi slowed down because of heat or power.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A metric can be restricted to one suite with <code>@suite</code>, e.g. <code>ttft_ms_p95@synthetic-512x128</code>, in gates and selection.</p>

<h2 id="evaluators">evaluators</h2>
<p>Every check scores an answer from 0 to 1 and decides pass or fail. The <a href="/lab/suites">suites</a> tab has the same list with ready-to-use examples.</p>
<table>
  <tbody>
