<h2 id="config">configuration</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>variable</th><th>default</th><th>purpose</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>LLB_HOME</code></td><td><code>~/.local/share/llmbench</code></td><td>Builds, llama.cpp source, lab state and results.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LLB_MODELS_DIR</code></td><td><code>./models</code></td><td>Favorites folder.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LLB_LLAMA_REF</code></td><td>set by each profile</td><td>llama.cpp version used by the profiles.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>HF_HOME</code>, <code>HF_TOKEN</code></td><td>Hugging Face defaults</td><td>Model cache and access to gated repos.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Optional extras for <code>uv sync</code>: <code>report</code> (HTML and PNG reports), <code>serve</code> (the dashboard and this lab), <code>hf-datasets</code> (suites from Hugging Face datasets or parquet files), <code>lm-eval</code> (the <code>lm_eval</code> phase). <code>uv sync</code> removes any extra you don't list, so repeat all of them every time. The <a href="/lab/system">system</a> tab shows which ones are installed.</p>

<h2 id="remote">remote access</h2>
<p>The lab listens on 127.0.0.1 only, because it has no authentication and can download and run things. To reach it from your other devices, use an SSH tunnel or Tailscale, which adds HTTPS and lets in only your own devices:</p>
<pre>sudo tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:8765   # https://&lt;machine&gt;.&lt;tailnet&gt;.ts.net/lab</pre>
<p>To keep it running on a Pi, a systemd user service is enough (<code>sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER</code> once, so it keeps running after you log out):</p>
<pre># ~/.config/systemd/user/llb-lab.service, then: systemctl --user enable --now llb-lab
[Unit]
Description=llb lab

[Service]
WorkingDirectory=%h/local-llm-bench-lab
ExecStart=%h/.local/bin/uv run --no-sync llb lab
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target</pre>

<h2 id="rules">rules the lab follows</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>One heavy thing at a time.</b> A run, a judge pass or a build first unloads the chat (a loaded model takes RAM and heats the CPU) and waits for downloads. While it runs, the lab refuses to load the chat, download or delete.</li>
  <li><b>Jobs survive the page and the lab.</b> Closing the browser does nothing to a run, and a restarted lab finds it again. <b>cancel</b> stops it like Ctrl-C: what was measured is kept, and <code>--resume</code> continues.</li>
  <li><b>Same files as the terminal.</b> The test and suites tabs edit <code>experiments/</code> and <code>suites/</code>; the form keeps the YAML's comments. Edits are saved only when you press <b>save</b> (run, judge and report save first).</li>
  <li><b>Try it is not a measurement.</b> It sends a few requests to the chat model with the chat's settings and stores nothing. Measurements are experiments.</li>
  <li><b>Deleting asks first.</b> Deleting from the Hugging Face cache removes the whole revision (the lab lists the other files it holds); <b>prune</b> deletes only what its preview showed.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="limits">limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>GPU</b>: llb builds llama.cpp with CUDA, Vulkan or Metal and passes every offloading flag, but it doesn't read GPU telemetry (VRAM, utilization, temperature, power). GPU builds are not tested on real hardware yet.</li>
  <li><b>Memory</b>: a run does not check in advance whether a model fits in RAM (the models tab does, before downloading). A run killed for lack of memory is recorded as failed, and the experiment continues.</li>
  <li><b>Scope</b>: llama.cpp on Linux only. macOS is untested, and there is no Ollama, vLLM, cloud APIs or energy measurement.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="problems">problems</h2>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The chat says there is no build</td><td>Compile one in <a href="/lab/system">system</a>, or start the lab with <code>--llama-server &lt;path&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A model is "too big" or the chat fails to load it</td><td>It does not fit in RAM: take a smaller quantization, or lower <b>context</b> in the chat (the KV cache grows with it).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A Pi run refuses to start</td><td>Set the governor: <code>echo performance | sudo tee /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_governor</code>. If it says throttled, improve the cooling or the power supply.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Runs wait a long time between them</td><td>That is the cooldown: the <code>pi5</code> profile waits for 60 °C. Use an active cooler.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A repo is gated or private</td><td>Accept its license on huggingface.co and start the lab with <code>HF_TOKEN</code> set.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A repo shows no quantizations</td><td>It has no GGUF files. Search for the model's name plus <code>GGUF</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Results marked <b>unstable</b></td><td>The repetitions varied more than 5% or the CPU throttled. Close other programs and run again (<code>--resume</code> keeps the rest).</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Something else</td><td>The job's <b>output</b> panel shows the command's full log; validate and plan show <code>llb</code>'s own messages.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
